{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Retret. Raymond III became count when he was very young after his father was assassinated. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III inherited a county that was often at odds with the Muslim powers surrounding it especially Nur ad-Din.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a key figure in the creation of the royal committee that ruled Jerusalem during the minority of Baldwin IV. Raymond III was also a rival of Guy of Lusignan who later became king of Jerusalem. He opposed Guy’s leadership and was instrumental in the Battle of Montgisard where Saladin’s forces were defeated in 1177. Raymond III also mediated disputes between the Crusader states and sought to strengthen alliances with the Byzantine Empire.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III’s reign was marked by constant warfare and shifting alliances. He fought against Nur ad-Din and later Saladin who sought to unify Muslim forces against the Crusaders. Raymond III’s strategic prowess was evident in his defense of Tripoli and his alliances with other Crusader leaders. He also played a crucial role in the Battle of Hattin where Saladin decisively defeated the Crusader army. Raymond III was one of the few leaders to escape the battle and he retreated to Tyre.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\nRaymond III’s later years were marked by his continued resistance against Saladin’s advances. He allied with Conrad of Montferrat to defend Tyre and prevent Saladin from capturing the city. Raymond III’s efforts were crucial in maintaining a Crusader foothold in the region. He died in 1187 leaving behind a legacy of resilience and strategic leadership. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which soon fell to Saladin’s forces. Raymond III’s contributions to the Crusader states were significant and his leadership helped delay the Muslim conquest of the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.19472213089466095}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -6.615896563744172e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.032347965985536575}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -8.523101132595912e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.30399298667907715}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06083328649401665}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.13382354559144e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00019643761334009469}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.2647828161716461}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.4533786177635193}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -5.483612312673358e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0006741396500729024}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00037222131504677236}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.821448318485636e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -9.095255518332124e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0005216429708525538}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0002609150833450258}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.632707417011261}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.005958057940006256}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.002696055569686e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.0874729418428615e-05}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -3.3854863431770355e-05}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -2.6464111215318553e-05}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.05153769254684448}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.009097079746425152}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.05306128039956093}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.08247550576925278}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.0011948358733206987}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.748573807475623e-06}, {"token": 